 "findings": [
  {
   "heading": "The **here** package",
   "segment_index": "S4.P1",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/importing.qmd:81-81",
   "tr_span": "chapters/importing.pt.qmd:77-77",
   "proposition": "The English states that a real copy of the raw linelist is first materialised into the \"data/linelists\" sub-folder with appliedepidata::save_data(), so the code below actually runs as written; the Portuguese drops this sentence while keeping the save_data() call in the chunk.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Portuguese reader sees an unexplained appliedepidata::save_data() line in the chunk and does not learn that the file must be created before import(here(...)) can succeed, so the example fails with a file-not-found error and the reader cannot tell why.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "The **here** package",
   "segment_index": "S4.P2",
   "kind": "code_mismatch",
   "en_span": "chapters/importing.qmd:89-93",
   "tr_span": "chapters/importing.pt.qmd:85-89",
   "proposition": "The Portuguese prose says the command here(\"data\", \"linelists\", \"ebola_linelist.xlsx\") produces the full path, but the chunk immediately above uses \"linelist_raw.xlsx\" and the path displayed immediately below ends in \"linelist_raw.xlsx\"; the English names \"linelist_raw.xlsx\" consistently.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Portuguese reader trying to reproduce the example types a file name that appears nowhere in the code and does not match the shown output path, and cannot tell which of the two file names here() is supposed to be given.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "\"Relative\" file paths",
   "segment_index": "S6.P1",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/importing.qmd:119-119",
   "tr_span": "chapters/importing.pt.qmd:115-115",
   "proposition": "The English adds that the reader must materialise the .xlsx file with appliedepidata::save_data() as shown above before the relative-path example will work; the Portuguese omits this instruction.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Portuguese reader assumes the example runs against a file that already exists, runs import(here(...)), and gets a file-not-found error with no pointer to the step that creates the file.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Authentication required",
   "segment_index": "S34.P2",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/importing.qmd:786-786",
   "tr_span": "chapters/importing.pt.qmd:765-765",
   "proposition": "The English CAUTION states that a large authenticated API import may TIME OUT; the Portuguese renders this as \"podera haver um intervalo de tempo\" (there may be a time interval), which does not state that the request fails.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Portuguese reader does not learn that the failure mode is a timed-out request, so the two remedies given right after (re-fetch the access_token before each GET, and filter or limit the query) have no stated problem to solve and are likely ignored.",
   "confidence": "medium"
  },
  {
   "heading": "With **rio** package",
   "segment_index": "S36.P2",
   "kind": "code_mismatch",
   "en_span": "chapters/importing.qmd:812-812",
   "tr_span": "chapters/importing.pt.qmd:791-791",
   "proposition": "The Portuguese prose says the csv file path is built with the function aqui(), but the chunk beside it calls here(); aqui() is not an R function and appears nowhere in the code.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Portuguese reader may believe the here package exports a Portuguese-named function and call aqui(), which errors with \"could not find function\"; at minimum the named function does not match the code shown.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Saving plots",
   "segment_index": "S40.P1",
   "kind": "untrue",
   "en_span": "chapters/importing.qmd:871-871",
   "tr_span": "chapters/importing.pt.qmd:850-850",
   "proposition": "The Portuguese says the plots are \"criados pelo pacote ggplot()\" (created by the ggplot() PACKAGE); the English says \"such as those created by ggplot()\", which is a function, not a package.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Portuguese reader is told ggplot() is a package name and may try to install or library() a package called ggplot, which does not exist; the package is ggplot2 and ggplot() is one of its functions.",
   "confidence": "medium"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Resources",
